  "summary": "The Instron universal testing system can perform compression tests on your cement cylinders to determine the load at failure. You will need to ensure that compression platens and a load cell with an appropriate capacity for your samples are available."
}

{
  "measurement_needs": [
    "Crystallization temperature during cooling from the melt"
  ],
  "objective_met": true,
  "recommendations": [
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ18",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC)",
      "why": "DSC measures the heat flow into or out of a sample as a function of temperature, allowing you to precisely identify the exothermic peak that occurs when the polymer crystallizes from the melt.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Appropriate sample pans and lids (e.g., standard aluminum)",
        "Cooling accessory (e.g., refrigerated cooling system) capable of controlled cooling from the polymer's melt temperature"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "To determine the crystallization temperature of your new polypropylene formulation, you can use Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC). The DSC will measure the heat released as the polymer crystallizes during a controlled cooling cycle from the melt, allowing you to pinpoint the exact temperature at which this occurs."
}

{
  "measurement_needs": [
    "Enthalpy of fusion (heat of melting) to calculate the degree of crystallinity of a polymer"
  ],
  "objective_met": true,
  "recommendations": [
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ18",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC)",
      "why": "DSC measures the heat absorbed when the crystalline regions of the PEEK melt. By comparing this measured heat of fusion to the known value for a 100% crystalline PEEK reference, you can calculate the exact percentage of crystallinity.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Appropriate sample pans and lids (e.g., standard aluminum or Tzero pans)",
        "Sample encapsulation press"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [
    {
      "technique": "X-ray Diffraction (XRD)",
      "why_useful": "XRD can directly quantify the ratio of crystalline diffraction peaks to the amorphous halo, providing an orthogonal method to verify the thermal results from DSC."
    }
  ],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "To get a quantitative number for the degree of crystallinity of your PEEK material, Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC) is the standard and most direct method. It will measure the energy required to melt the crystalline portions of your samples, allowing you to calculate the crystalline percentage and track how your processing conditions affect it."
}

{
  "measurement_needs": [
    "Elongation at break",
    "Ultimate tensile strength (force at failure)"
  ],
  "objective_met": true,
  "recommendations": [
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ15",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Uniaxial tensile testing",
      "why": "This system can apply controlled tension to the elastic chains and measure the force and displacement until they snap.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Tensile grips suitable for elastomers",
        "Appropriate capacity load cell (e.g., 100 N or 1 kN)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "A universal mechanical testing system is ideal for determining the maximum stretch and breaking force of your orthodontic chains. By performing a tensile test to failure, you can accurately compare the elongation at break across the three brands. Be sure to confirm that suitable tensile grips and a low-capacity load cell are available for testing small elastic samples."
}

{
  "measurement_needs": [
    "Weight change as a function of temperature to determine the residual mass after organic components decompose"
  ],
  "objective_met": true,
  "recommendations": [
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ17",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA)",
      "why": "TGA continuously measures the mass of a sample as it is heated. By heating the composite paste to a high temperature, the organic resin matrix will decompose and volatilize, leaving only the inorganic filler behind to be weighed.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "High-temperature sample pans (e.g., platinum or alumina)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "To determine the inorganic filler fraction by weight, you need to burn off the organic resin matrix and weigh the remaining material. A Thermogravimetric Analyzer (TGA) is the ideal instrument for this, as it precisely measures weight loss as the sample is heated. Note that this is a destructive test, as the organic portion of the composite paste will be completely degraded during the measurement."
}

