  "summary": "Your microbeads are too large for standard dynamic light scattering (DLS) instruments like the listed Zetasizer, which typically max out around 10 µm. Instead, optical microscopy combined with image analysis is the best available approach. The Keyence high-content imaging system can automate the imaging and sizing process, while standard inverted or stereo microscopes can be used to capture images for manual or software-based analysis. A laser diffraction analyzer would be ideal for rapid bulk measurements but is not currently listed."
}

{
  "measurement_needs": [
    "Quantification of mass loss due to evaporation of water and other volatiles upon heating"
  ],
  "objective_met": true,
  "recommendations": [
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ17",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA)",
      "why": "TGA continuously measures the mass of a sample as it is heated, allowing you to precisely quantify the amount of water and other volatiles driven off during the temperature ramp.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Appropriate sample pans (e.g., platinum or alumina)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ18",
      "role": "supplementary",
      "technique": "Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC)",
      "why": "DSC measures heat flow and can identify the specific temperatures at which endothermic evaporation events occur, providing complementary thermal data to the TGA mass loss.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Appropriate sample pans (e.g., pinhole or hermetically sealed pans)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [
    {
      "technique": "Karl Fischer Titration",
      "why_useful": "While TGA measures total volatile mass loss, it cannot chemically distinguish water from other residual solvents. Karl Fischer titration specifically and exclusively quantifies water content."
    }
  ],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "To measure the volatile content of your hygroscopic powder, Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) is the primary technique. The TGA550 will heat your sample and record the exact mass lost as water and other volatiles evaporate. You can pair this with DSC to observe the thermal energy associated with these evaporation events. Keep in mind that TGA measures total mass loss; if you need to specifically isolate water content from other potential residual solvents, a Karl Fischer titrator (which is not currently listed) would be necessary."
}

{
  "measurement_needs": [
    "High-resolution imaging of individual nanoparticles",
    "Determination of nanoparticle morphology (shape) and physical dimensions"
  ],
